::ENG3_U03_L06_GP01::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: People of the city, look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children waiting at the market. If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. What rhetorical choice appears in "look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children"? {
~A definition of grain prices.#No definition is given.
=Repetition.#Correct. The phrase "look at" is repeated to focus attention.
~A list of laws.#The passage lists images, not laws.
~A personal anecdote.#No personal story is told.
}

::ENG3_U03_L06_GP02::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: People of the city, look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children waiting at the market. If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. What effect does the repetition create? {
~It makes the problem seem distant and unimportant.#The repeated images make the problem immediate.
~It hides the speaker's purpose.#It makes the purpose clearer.
=It builds urgency by forcing the audience to picture several signs of need.#Correct. The repetition creates pressure and urgency.
~It proves that the city has enough food.#The images show shortage.
}

::ENG3_U03_L06_GP03::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. What rhetorical choice is used in this sentence? {
~A neutral statistic.#No statistic appears.
~A direct quotation from an expert.#No expert is quoted.
~A chronological sequence.#No time sequence is developed.
=Personification.#Correct. Hunger is described as if it can speak.
}

::ENG3_U03_L06_GP04::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: People of the city, look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children waiting at the market. If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. What is the author's likely purpose? {
=To persuade the city to act quickly on the grain shipment.#Correct. The images and warning push for immediate action.
~To entertain citizens with a story about a market.#The passage is persuasive, not mainly entertaining.
~To explain how mills are built.#The mill is an image, not a construction topic.
~To argue against feeding children.#The passage presents children's need as urgent.
}

::ENG3_U03_L06_GP05::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: People of the city, look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children waiting at the market. If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. Which evaluation is strongest? {
~The rhetoric is effective because it has many words.#Word count is not an evaluation of effect.
~The rhetoric is ineffective because it mentions hunger.#Mentioning hunger supports the urgent purpose.
=The rhetoric is effective because repetition and personification make the food shortage feel immediate and serious.#Correct. This connects choices to effect and purpose.
~The rhetoric is effective because it avoids emotion completely.#The passage uses emotional images deliberately.
}
